[4] Sum-shadow bridges and code/LRC beacons
    L2(n)=S1(m) hits n,m<=500: [(3, 4, 90)]
    R2(n)=S1(m) hits n,m<=500: []
    The unique checked hit is L2(3)=90=S1(4).
    At the same row, R2(3)=78=C(13,2), the existing [72,36,16] lambda_5 beacon.
      value 25: [('S2', 1)]
      value 27: [('R2', 2)]
      value 42: [('S1', 3)]
      value 78: [('R2', 3)]
      value 90: [('L2', 3), ('S1', 4)]
      value 91: []
      value 165: [('S1', 5)]
      value 365: [('S2', 2)]
      value 2030: [('S2', 3)]
      value 7230: [('S2', 4)]
      value 249849: []

    Interpretation: row Q(3) is not a code construction, but it creates a
    tight additive/square shadow: 21+22+23+24 has ordinary sum 90 while
    25+26+27 has ordinary sum 78, and the squared equality binds them.

